[Applause]  at the angel in burford it's too late to  offer salvation to the christmas trade  concern about omicron as slash custom  and takings in this corner of the  cotswolds as it has across the country  leaving pubs half empty and the brewery  with more beer than they can sell in  what should be the busiest time of year  it's been pretty grim really you know we  were looking forward to a good december  because december's very much the time  that hospitality hopefully gets someone  in the bank to see them through the  darker days of january and february but  the last two three weeks have been  really really tough bookings cancelled  across the across the whole landscape a  fortnight after omicron began to hit  trade the under pressure chancellor  announced one billion pounds worth of  support including grants and sick pay  for hospitality businesses and the  promise of more if lockdowns return  now of course  i will always respond proportionately  and appropriately to the situation that  we face people can have confidence in  that but where we are now we've  responded i think generously today the  grants that we've outlined up to 6 000  pounds are comparable to the grants that  we provided for hospitality businesses  when they were completely closed earlier  this year  the angels licensee gemma it's welcome  but what she really wants to know is if  restrictions around the corner well i  mean it's better than nothing for sure  um but to be honest it just doesn't  touch the sides for our business  businesses like us and for us i think  with a 40 reduction in revenue which i  don't think makes this unique i think  that's quite typical across the board  we're losing a huge amount more than 6  000 pounds  the measures set a precedent by offering  financial support to businesses hit not  by forced closures but the collapse in  consumer confidence this one billion  pound package is a fraction of that  which was offered earlier in the  pandemic but it's justified by the